{"title":"Rational approximation","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Introduction\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Introduction</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Trivial_theorem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Trivial theorem</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Proof\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Dirichlet&#39;s_theorem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Dirichlet's theorem</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Proof_of_Dirichlet&#39;s_theorem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof of Dirichlet's theorem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Corollary\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Corollary</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Proof_of_the_corollary\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof of the corollary</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Discussion\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Discussion</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Applications_to_problem_solving\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Applications to problem solving</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Liouville_Approximation_Theorem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Liouville Approximation Theorem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Hurwitz&#39;s_theorem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Hurwitz's theorem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Roth&#39;s_theorem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Roth's theorem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Introduction\">Introduction</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Introduction\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The main theme of this article is the question how well a given <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Real_number\" title=\"Real number\">real number</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> can be approximated by <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_number\" title=\"Rational number\">rational numbers</a>. Of course, since the rationals are dense on the real line, we, surely, can make the difference between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and its rational approximation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/0/8c08adadeb0c9372654ee5cc432ccb32f255443b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac pq$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"11\" height=\"36\" /> as small as we wish. The problem is that, as we try to make <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/0/8c08adadeb0c9372654ee5cc432ccb32f255443b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac pq$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"11\" height=\"36\" /> closer and closer to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, we may have to use larger and larger <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />. So, the reasonable question to ask here is how well can we approximate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> by rationals with not too large denominators.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Trivial_theorem\">Trivial theorem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Trivial theorem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Every real number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> can be approximated by a rational number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/4/7347a1d5586e82c832f61ea799d89945ed2837fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{p}{q}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"11\" height=\"36\" /> with a given denominator <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/3/7f3795c30541e78f81039b5bc5cec9b300d4ef6e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q\\ge 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"41\" height=\"15\" /> with an error not exceeding <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/1/711754da0edb189cf89502ba486fd7bbeaaffa4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac 1{2q}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"20\" height=\"40\" />. \n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Note that the closed interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/3/4434ff4e3265c50511c19f182f9011a52005b5ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left[qx-\\frac12,qx+\\frac12\\right]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"131\" height=\"43\" /> has length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and, therefore, contains at least one integer. Choosing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> to be that integer, we immediately get the result.\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>So, the interesting question is whether we can get a smaller error of approximation than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/f/a0f452c058ec4125301812cb91cefdbeac7cd2af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac 1q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"15\" height=\"42\" />. Surprisingly enough, it is possible, if not for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, then, at least for some of them.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Dirichlet.27s_theorem\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Dirichlet's_theorem\">Dirichlet's theorem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Dirichlet&#39;s theorem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/6/0965ac6a39c0b4d67771c31210b98312ae2e4f01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\ge 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" /> be any integer. Then there exists a rational number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/0/8c08adadeb0c9372654ee5cc432ccb32f255443b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac pq$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"11\" height=\"36\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/a/ffabf9de7b85dba79503693b417d89e67a6696d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\le q\\le n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"77\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/2/d82b8f70234f99a81ff5330c8c0d1bcb2b7aacc0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left|x-\\frac pq\\right|&lt;\\frac 1{nq}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"104\" height=\"43\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span id=\"Proof_of_Dirichlet.27s_theorem\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof_of_Dirichlet's_theorem\">Proof of Dirichlet's theorem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Proof of Dirichlet&#39;s theorem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Consider the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Fractional_part\" title=\"Fractional part\">fractional parts</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/6/a16edeb8c08d38f655c4f1595737e9fce4ee7bc0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{0\\cdot x\\},\\{1\\cdot x\\}, \\{2\\cdot x\\},\\dots, \\{n\\cdot x\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"263\" height=\"20\" />. They all belong to the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Half-open_interval\" title=\"Half-open interval\">half-open interval</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1a809324034256fb4541c3b1d336e005d488ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" />. Represent the interval <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1a809324034256fb4541c3b1d336e005d488ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" /> as the union of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> subintervals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/3/023f86ce649dbe59ace76f378763c30c7e29cbab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0,1)=\\left[0,\\frac 1n\\right)\\cup\\left[\\frac 1n,\\frac 2n\\right)\\cup\\dots\\cup\\left[\\frac{n-1}{n},1\\right),$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"352\" height=\"43\" />. Since we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> fractional parts and only <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> subintervals, the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Pigeonhole_Principle\" title=\"Pigeonhole Principle\">pigeonhole principle</a> implies that there are two integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/d/08d31729ade6f61363ca8c28c09312591580c3d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0\\le k&lt;\\ell\\le n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"110\" height=\"15\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/a/3fa6ac2c40f9e0e7116dc13bce075ff73e3e927e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{kx\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/1/101cdba43c1b6fb50110d9c899cfe445b22e94db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{\\ell x\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"38\" height=\"20\" /> belong to the same interval. But then the difference <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/9/bf98c3aadf0e3cfe82507cfb3c7feced7a6fb787.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\ell-k)x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"64\" height=\"18\" /> differs by less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/e/0ee06e6776df89765d19b1644085a0100065f4b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac 1n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"13\" height=\"37\" /> from some integer number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/7/ab785ac526fdc865f812333794cc6e188b4e2f2f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|(\\ell-k)x-p|&lt;\\frac 1n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"147\" height=\"38\" />. Dividing by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/d/b3d72bb1c8e66e0d2f7a489a00e5b14ceff172cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q=\\ell-k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"72\" height=\"16\" />, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/3/aa35465485d9706032bc3cca3a6224517b9438de.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left|x-\\frac pq\\right|&lt;\\frac1{nq}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"107\" height=\"45\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Corollary\">Corollary</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Corollary\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Irrational_number\" title=\"Irrational number\">irrational</a>, then there are infinitely many irreducible fractions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/0/8c08adadeb0c9372654ee5cc432ccb32f255443b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac pq$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"11\" height=\"36\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/d/9bd4df44fdfd5e5d1ddf609da4a957f5650a2e04.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left|x-\\frac pq\\right|&lt;\\frac 1{q^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"100\" height=\"43\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof_of_the_corollary\">Proof of the corollary</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Proof of the corollary\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>For each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/6/0965ac6a39c0b4d67771c31210b98312ae2e4f01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\ge 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" />, find a (possibly reducible) fraction <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/3/f23fc3d101b7001d2c055bdead23d6fa3f4097fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {P_n}{Q_n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"25\" height=\"41\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/a/c5aebc8714ea710e6f62b78ddef4527659d12421.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\le Q_n\\le n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"91\" height=\"16\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/1/4c197aecb8fc92dbd96fc13741921c446fcfabd9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left|x-\\frac {P_n}{Q_n}\\right|&lt;\\frac 1{nQ_n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"132\" height=\"43\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/3/1538a6631d0bfeba470147f470f69e4a12cebf84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {p_n}{q_n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"20\" height=\"36\" /> be the same fraction as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/3/f23fc3d101b7001d2c055bdead23d6fa3f4097fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {P_n}{Q_n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"25\" height=\"41\" /> but reduced to its lowest terms. It is clear that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/3/3d309aa4bb4a9d4fbdd06f5bf81197b47e46ad60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac 1{nQ_n}\\le \\frac 1{Q_n^2}\\le \\frac 1{q_n^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"133\" height=\"41\" />, so it remains to show that among the fractions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/3/1538a6631d0bfeba470147f470f69e4a12cebf84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {p_n}{q_n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"20\" height=\"36\" /> there are infinitely many different ones. But the distance from the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />-th fraction to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> does not exceed <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/e/0ee06e6776df89765d19b1644085a0100065f4b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac 1n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"13\" height=\"37\" />, which can be made arbitrarily small  if we take large enough <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. On the other hand, if the fractions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/3/1538a6631d0bfeba470147f470f69e4a12cebf84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {p_n}{q_n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"20\" height=\"36\" /> were finitely many, this distance couldn't be made less than the distance from the irrational number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> to some finite set of rational numbers,  i.e., less than some positive constant.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Discussion\">Discussion</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Discussion\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>The Dirichlet's theorem can be generalized in various ways. The first way is that one can try to approximate several numbers simultaneously by fractions with common denominator. The exact statement is as follows. \n</p>\n<hr />\n<p><i>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/a/04a182c41a392ec852c2a457b11feadb1cf10f4d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1,\\dots,x_m\\in \\mathbb R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"116\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/6/0965ac6a39c0b4d67771c31210b98312ae2e4f01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\ge 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" /> is an integer, then there exists an integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/b/0ab6bfda1a085492242ab1b70471ac08587b25b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\le q\\le n^m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"89\" height=\"16\" /> and integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/0/350c94f2ebdfe9a5fd9f0724f7ea30f15a280fb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_1,\\dots,p_m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"78\" height=\"11\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/a/acac873891c6244e80faf55228ea0b0e4025c425.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left|x_j-\\frac {p_j}q\\right|&lt;\\frac 1{nq}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"121\" height=\"45\" /></i>\n</p>\n<hr />\n<p>The proof is essentially the same except instead of considering <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/a/3fa6ac2c40f9e0e7116dc13bce075ff73e3e927e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{kx\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/9/5/39555a530ccc907d03d427c7cca19db604634a36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=0,\\dots,n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"94\" height=\"16\" />one has to consider <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/b/04b80b03e8cea3a7bbf1b7bde6e585f2680d31fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n^m+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"53\" height=\"14\" /> vectors <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/c/20c81d240971c265a84359a7dcf7ca4eaf93d4fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\{kx_1\\},\\dots,\\{kx_m\\})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"150\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/d/f2dcb375ae039586deefa1c1457e6ded4616f1ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k=0,\\dots, n^m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"106\" height=\"16\" /> in the unit cube <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/2/d5214eac4e9fccdf9d42956cadedddb3dfb6c497.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0,1)^m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"49\" height=\"18\" /> divided into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/6/11635427dac1ad5c3e57beb5e3dfcb329a78248b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n^m$\" width=\"22\" height=\"12\" /> equal subcubes.\n</p><p>Another remark that can be useful in some problems is that, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is irrational, then you can find infinitely many solutions of the inequality <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/2/de2663dec0cf2ab9bce219c44a7bceeb2a75461d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left|x-\\frac {p}q\\right|&lt;\\frac C{q^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"100\" height=\"43\" /> with the denominator <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> contained in any given arithmetic progression <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/a/86a1bc968c7d0ce4111854bab99561fff84b71a1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a\\ell+b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"47\" height=\"14\" /> (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/3/2039f4954270edecb0359fd982d8dfac8cc5c396.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ell\\in\\mathbb Z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"41\" height=\"14\" />) if the constant <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> (depending on the progression) is large enough. To prove it, first, find infinitely many irreducible fractions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/5/5550fbf6499555355281bdccea521357cd509b9a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac PQ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"16\" height=\"41\" /> satisfying <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/8/14869ab4cfa325873750d520f4a69a48a699446c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left|x-\\frac PQ\\right|&lt;\\frac 1{Q^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"111\" height=\"43\" />. Then, for each such fraction, find two integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/0/5f03710a9ffbf0ecba4ae631d6f3bd28ff7f48f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u,v$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"27\" height=\"11\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/3/123d7666e2d20afd4525d65d6573d8789d1eca40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0&lt; u\\le Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"81\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/1/0a1824a401e40729f1eedb66f8656cf53718b41c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$uP+vQ=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"103\" height=\"16\" />. Now note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad99798ec4c38e165cf517cb9e02b1c9e824103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/6/9866e3a998d628ba0941eb4fea0666ac391d149a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" /> are relatively prime, so we can find some integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/0/70023895dee56cef9e36a02cc119e0d61365da55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\alpha, \\beta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"30\" height=\"16\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/0/e1048feafde7ed3039b58f5ac65ac5904a84e449.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\alpha u+\\beta Q=b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"102\" height=\"16\" />. Replacing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/f/10f32377ac67d94f764f12a15ea987e88c85d3e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\alpha$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/b/fdb63b9e51abe6bbb16acfb5d7b773ddbb5bf4a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\beta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" /> by their <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Remainder\" title=\"Remainder\">remainders</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/b/fbb49d13251b6c8c7c475a77d132f318bb8019b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tilde\\alpha$\" width=\"11\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/1/4a1bd27291ea539c6d91ec016308a46710e785d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tilde\\beta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"20\" /> modulo <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, we get a positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/b/b9b6966c51ee763216a71e72e2b5b8a05ceed692.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q=\\tilde\\alpha u+\\tilde\\beta Q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"102\" height=\"20\" /> satisfying <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/a/09af54a89801707a6f8180b8971cdaa43d293529.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\le q\\le 2aQ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"98\" height=\"16\" /> and  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/c/d6c56cc2781600697e7e8df2efa8f57909c800fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|qx-(\\tilde\\beta P-\\tilde\\alpha v)|\\le\\tilde\\alpha|ux+v|+\\tilde\\beta|Qx-P|\\le \\tilde\\alpha u\\left|x-\\frac PQ\\right|+\\frac{\\tilde\\alpha}Q+\\frac {\\tilde\\beta}Q\\le \\frac {3a}Q\\le \\frac {6a^2}q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"630\" height=\"46\" />. Thus, setting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/0/bc0f02bf122c25a3cc91238e67286caf1333cfa8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p=\\tilde\\beta P-\\tilde\\alpha v$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"103\" height=\"20\" />, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/f/5ef6c1682fbddf172909c9e9f00bd2b760b2bf69.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left|x-\\frac pq\\right|&lt;\\frac {6a^2}{q^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"110\" height=\"43\" />.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Applications_to_problem_solving\">Applications to problem solving</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Applications to problem solving\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>One common way to apply Dirichlet's theorem in problem solving is to use it in the following form: given finitely many numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/3/0c336386f3b669f545fbec1d2f6700e69d9cbfa8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1,\\dots,x_m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"80\" height=\"11\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/d/c6da49fefb1906c55ed166a63282b0d8e19ca31c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\delta&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"41\" height=\"13\" />, one can find a positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> such that each of the numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/e/eceab0144cb7d6a482de765c78d7749d38ce8642.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$qx_1, qx_2,\\dots, qx_m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"132\" height=\"11\" /> differs from some integer by less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/4/0a42a8b725c200085e5253db2d2fbf8dab898a8f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\delta$\" width=\"8\" height=\"13\" />. A typical example of such usage can be found in the article devoted to the famous <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Partition_of_a_rectangle_into_squares_problem\" title=\"Partition of a rectangle into squares problem\">Partition of a rectangle into squares problem</a>.  \n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Liouville_Approximation_Theorem\">Liouville Approximation Theorem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Liouville Approximation Theorem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can generalize Dirichlet's theorem as follows: If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/f/10f32377ac67d94f764f12a15ea987e88c85d3e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\alpha$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> is an <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Algebraic_number\" title=\"Algebraic number\">algebraic number</a> of degree <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, then there are only finitely many rational numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/4/7347a1d5586e82c832f61ea799d89945ed2837fc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{p}{q}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"11\" height=\"36\" /> satisfying the following inequality:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/d/37d3e6e431e8fbb5c1a9497fc6a10e360a0fc032.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Bigg|\\alpha-\\frac{p}{q}\\Bigg| \\le\\frac{1}{q^n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"103\" height=\"54\" />. This gives us the following corollary: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/0/6507d11c21180b7d183615862df531875f10f2b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty 10^{-n!}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"68\" height=\"48\" /> is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Transcendental_number\" title=\"Transcendental number\">transcendental number</a>, known as <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Liouville%27s_constant&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Liouville&#39;s constant (page does not exist)\">Liouville's constant</a>.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Hurwitz.27s_theorem\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Hurwitz's_theorem\">Hurwitz's theorem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Hurwitz&#39;s theorem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For every <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Irrational_number\" title=\"Irrational number\">irrational number</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/f/ccfd1641d037d427d9dbf42cdd54b485a03eafe9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\xi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"8\" height=\"16\" /> there are infinitely many <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_number\" title=\"Rational number\">rationals</a> <i>m</i>/<i>n</i> such that \n</p>\n<dl><dd><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/5/4758ed721e16091a5138c5354aaddbbbe2db45db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left |\\xi-\\frac{m}{n}\\right |&lt;\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}\\, n^2}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"140\" height=\"41\" /></dd></dl>\n<h2><span id=\"Roth.27s_theorem\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Roth's_theorem\">Roth's theorem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Roth&#39;s theorem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For algebraic &#945;, integers <i>p</i> and <i>q</i>;\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/3/f3359981b71e6fc6563f227590a6e4ad1c9035ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|\\alpha-p/q|&lt;1/q^{2+\\epsilon}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"144\" height=\"19\" />\nhas finitely many solutions as &#949;&gt;0.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Rational_approximation_of_famous_numbers\" title=\"Rational approximation of famous numbers\">Rational approximation of famous numbers</a>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017234852\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.048 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.058 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 581/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 19633/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:2056:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017234852 and revision id 106026. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}